# Factor Graphs

!slide Definition ^fg_def

A **factor graph** is a bipartite graph $G = (V \cup F, E)$ over variables $V$
and factors $F$, encoding a factorization

:: math
	p(x_1, \dots, x_n) \;=\; \frac{1}{Z} \prod_{a \in F} f_a(x_{\partial a}),
	\qquad Z = \sum_{x} \prod_{a \in F} f_a(x_{\partial a})

with $x_{\partial a}$ the variables adjacent to factor $a$ (see@kschischang2001).

!pause

!img The example graph, built up step by step ^fg_fig
!src figs/fg_layer1.svg
!src figs/fg_layer2.svg
!src figs/fg_layer3.svg
!caption Circles are variables, squares are factors.
!width 44%


!slide The sum-product update ^sum_product

Messages are exchanged along edges. Variable-to-factor and factor-to-variable:

:: math
	\mu_{x \to a}(x) \;=\; \prod_{b \in \partial x \setminus \{a\}} \mu_{b \to x}(x)

!pause

:: math
	\mu_{a \to x}(x) \;=\; \mk{sum}{\sum_{x_{\partial a} \setminus \{x\}}}
		\mk{fac}{f_a(x_{\partial a})} \mk{prod}{\prod_{y \in \partial a \setminus \{x\}} \mu_{y \to a}(y)}

!annotate
	prod: collect incoming messages
	fac[#7a4b94]: weigh by local factor
	sum: marginalize out everything except $x$

!pause

On trees this gives exact marginals; on loopy graphs it becomes an
__asynchronous fixed-point iteration__, and convergence is the interesting
question.

!notes
	Derivation of the tree case on the whiteboard: distributivity of + over *.
	Mention that scheduling matters on loopy graphs; bridge to my own work.


!slide Grouping: one name, two places ^grouping

A marginal likelihood weighs a **prior** against a **likelihood** — and both
terms reappear, in a new guise, once the Gaussian integral is written out:

:: math
	\begin{aligned}
	p(w \mid a, b)
	  &= \int \mk{prior}{p(\theta \mid a)} \; \mk{lik}{p(w \mid \theta, b)} \; d\theta \\
	  &= \int \mk{prior}{\mathcal{N}(\theta;\, \mu_a, \Sigma_a)} \;
	         \mk{lik}{\mathcal{N}(w;\, X\theta, b^{-1} I)} \; d\theta
	\end{aligned}

!annotate
	prior[#7a4b94]: the prior over $\theta$
	lik[#2e7d32]: the likelihood of $w$

Each name is used twice, so the two occurrences form a **group**: one colour
covers both, and the single label's arrow fans out to each — all on one keypress.


!slide Reference implementation

:: python[1|4-6|3,7] ^sp_code
	def msg_var_to_fac(x, a, inbox):
		"""Product of all incoming factor messages except the one from a."""
		out = np.ones(x.dim)
		for b in x.neighbors:
			if b is not a:
				out *= inbox[b, x]
		return out

!pause

Note the absence of a global schedule: `inbox` can be updated
**asynchronously**, which is the door to spike-based formulations.


!slide Exercise: compute by hand ^exercise

